    "reading": "Two costs dominate a spike and both are PER NEW CAR TYPE: reading/parsing its .osm (vehicle-osm, worst single 20.5 ms \u2014 'bus') and uploading it to the GPU (vehicle-model, worst single 18.2 ms \u2014 'tahoma'); typical types cost 0.5-2 ms each. A teleport pays 27-43 of them at once, which is a bench-only shape \u2014 in play a type appears one at a time. Cell collision is the other named cost (COL parse 9.6-78.3 ms, Rapier bodies 5.6-28.1 ms). unattributed still holds 40-55 % of a teleport spike and 100 % of the two frames that FOLLOW one (68.2 and 38.9 ms with no span open at all) \u2014 GC-shaped, and the honest next question rather than a fifth span.",
    "what": "Every [slow] line of the same run, which is what this run was taken for. `frame` is the REAL elapsed interval (the sim clamps dt at 250 ms; before 091 the line printed the clamp, so the boot frame read 250.0 when it was 576.1). `other` = the interval minus every timed block; the spans are what the between-frame work reported about itself; `unattributed` is the rest (GC + whatever nobody wrapped). ALL of these sit OUTSIDE the measured windows \u2014 they are settle/teleport frames, the known pattern."
